WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.112
Coding Exercise 2 asks you to create one summary row
per diagnosis

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.988
Start with the grain one row per diagnosis That
tells you diagnosis

00:00:10.012 --> 00:00:14.925
must appear in GROUP BY The measure is
admission_count so use COUNT

00:00:14.938 --> 00:00:18.650
star and give it that alias Then sort the result so
the

00:00:18.675 --> 00:00:23.312
largest count appears first Because the alias is
available for the final

00:00:23.338 --> 00:00:28.612
ORDER BY you can sort by admission_count descending
Read the finished query

00:00:28.638 --> 00:00:32.500
in plain language for each diagnosis count the
admission rows and display

00:00:32.513 --> 00:00:37.162
the diagnoses from most frequent to least frequent
Run and Check then

00:00:37.188 --> 00:00:42.838
look at the output Each diagnosis should appear once
If a diagnosis

00:00:42.862 --> 00:00:48.375
appears more than once the grouping is probably
incomplete If PostgreSQL returns

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.225
a grouping error check that every selected
non-aggregate column belongs in GROUP

00:00:52.238 --> 00:00:56.700
BY This is a small exercise but it represents the
central GROUP

00:00:56.725 --> 00:01:01.088
BY pattern used in many dashboards and reports
category plus aggregate measure

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:01.750
plus sorting
